## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design end-to-end small AWS AI pipeline with multilingual text handling, demonstrating practical integration of managed AI services with proper governance.

## Key Concepts

- Event-driven AWS pipelines using S3 triggers
- Amazon Translate and Comprehend service integration
- Data privacy: server-side encryption and CloudTrail access logging
- Robust error handling: Dead Letter Queues and retry mechanisms
- Cost management: translate pricing models, storage classes, and request optimization
- IAM least-privilege access patterns
- DynamoDB upsert operations for metadata indexing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Lambda handler for ticket processing
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const record = event.Records[0];
  const bucket = record.s3.bucket.name;
  const key = record.s3.object.key;

  // Fetch, translate, analyze, store, and index
  const original = await getS3Object(bucket, key);
  const translated = await translateIfNeeded(original);
  const analysis = await analyzeSentiment(translated);
  await storeResults(original, translated);
  await updateDynamoDB(record, analysis);
};
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test translation quality and handle edge cases?
- How would you scale to thousands of concurrent ticket uploads?
- What monitoring and alerting would you implement for production?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design end-to-end data pipelines in AWS using serverless components, data cataloging, and lightweight ETL operations while considering security, privacy, and cost optimization.

## Key Concepts
- S3 event triggers and Lambda orchestration
- AWS Glue Data Catalog and crawlers for schema inference
- Lightweight ETL transformations in Lambda (normalization, data validation)
- DynamoDB indexing with upsert operations for idempotency
- IAM roles with least-privilege access
- Encryption at rest (SSE-KMS) and in transit (TLS)
- S3 access logs for audit trails
- Retry mechanisms and dead-letter queues for error handling
- Cost considerations: Lambda invocation duration, Glue crawler runtime, S3 storage, and DynamoDB read/write capacity

## Code Example
